MLX's CUDA quantized matmul read the weights once per row of x for a few
rows, and once per batch of x always:

- On GPUs with qmm_sm80 (sm_80 and later, sm_120 included) it ran qmv for
  M*B < 8. qmv launches one block row per row of x, so every row streams the
  whole weight matrix again: 2..7 rows (speculative verify) cost about M
  times one row. qmm_sm80 reads the weights once for all rows and from two
  rows is as fast as qmv at one (sm_120: a 6-row 4096x12288 4-bit matmul
  485 -> 94 us). Use qmv for a single row only, as the sm90 branch does.
- x of shape [B, M, K] against a 2D weight ran as B problems of M rows, each
  a full pass over the weights: batched decode ([B, 1, K]) cost B single
  steps. Fold the batches into rows (a view, x is row-contiguous) so one
  pass serves them all.

Upstream PR pending (the user submits it); drop this file and its step in
build-mlx-linux.sh once lib/mlx-src includes it.

